## 1. Introduction

**Task:** Development of **Hira**, a web application based on a RAG architecture for automated job matching, targeting university graduates in the Republic of Belarus. Hira is both the product name and the assistant's name. Product surfaces (library, voice call, diploma boundary) are specified in `Wiki/11-hira.md`.

**Main Idea:**
Moving beyond single-branch semantic search. The system combines three complementary retrieval branches — lexical (BM25), dense (bi-encoder), and graph traversal over a skill knowledge graph — fused by Reciprocal Rank Fusion and re-ranked by a cross-encoder. An LLM handles query understanding (with dialogue context and long-term user memory) and answer generation, while relevance is explained through a deterministic, factor-wise decomposition rather than model-generated prose.

This replaces the 2026 solution (Streamlit + FastAPI + Sentence-BERT bi-encoder only) with a multi-user web application built on a microservice architecture.

**Tech Stack:**
- **Language:** TypeScript (strict mode, no implicit `any`)
- **Frontend:** Next.js 15 (App Router), React 19, Tailwind CSS v4, Zustand
- **Core backend:** Nest.js (auth, CRUD, orchestration, Swagger)
- **Microservices:** Express (`retrieval-service`, `llm-service`, `ingestion-service`)
- **ORM:** TypeORM
- **Database:** PostgreSQL 17 + pgvector + pg_trgm (local)
- **Neural core:** `BAAI/bge-m3` (1024d encoder) + `BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3` (568M cross-encoder, ADR-022), served via Hugging Face TEI on CPU. A ~278M reranker is an ablation only.
- **LLM:** OpenRouter (dev + prod); models with `json_schema` support, paid if needed
- **Cache & queues:** Redis (LLM cache, BullMQ, rate limiting)
- **Validation:** class-validator, class-transformer, Yup
- **Testing:** Vitest · **Tooling:** Docker Compose, ESLint, Prettier

**Hardware constraint:** the target machine is an Intel Core i9-9880H with 32 GB RAM and an AMD Radeon Pro 560X. Neither CUDA nor PyTorch MPS is available, so all neural inference runs on CPU. Production re-ranking still uses `bge-reranker-v2-m3` on the top-20 (ADR-022): quality outranks latency. Several seconds per query on this CPU is accepted.

