## 3. Functional Requirements

- **Multi-source ingestion:** Connectors for rabota.by (official HeadHunter REST API), the state employment portal (gsz.gov.by), Habr Career, praca.by, LinkedIn, and Indeed, on a cron schedule, with cross-source deduplication and expiry detection. The two English-language sources are rate-limited aggressively, restricted to public pages, and collected ahead of time so that demonstrations run against a database snapshot.
- **Bilingual corpus, English UI:** Russian and English vacancies coexist; FTS configuration is per document language; skill aliases in both languages; foreign currencies converted to BYN; **UI is English-only**; Hira answers in the language of the user's message (ADR-028). Dark theme only (blue–dark-blue–black palette).
- **Geography:** Minimum coverage — all of Belarus; EU/US/world if resources allow; foreign remote jobs flagged with UI filter.
- **Freelance and project work:** Modelled explicitly as employment types with hourly/daily/per-project rates and an optional project duration, rather than being forced into a monthly-salary schema.
- **Skill extraction and normalization:** LLM-based extraction of required skills from vacancy descriptions, canonicalized against the ESCO taxonomy (labels machine-translated into Russian, with manual review of the upper hierarchy and the most frequent skills) through an alias table with fuzzy and vector fallback linking.
- **Skill knowledge graph:** Nodes for skills, occupations, companies, locations; edges for `REQUIRES`, `ALTERNATIVE_OF`, `BROADER`/`NARROWER`, `RELATED_TO` (weighted by corpus co-occurrence), `TYPICALLY_REQUIRES`, `TRANSITIONS_TO`.
- **Query understanding:** LLM converts a raw message plus dialogue history plus retrieved memory into a structured `SearchIntent` with a rewritten self-contained query, hard/soft constraints, and a query-complexity class.
- **Hybrid retrieval:** Three parallel branches (lexical, dense, graph), fused by RRF with branch weights adapted to query complexity.
- **Adaptive constraint relaxation:** Soft constraints are dropped in ascending order of weight until the candidate pool is sufficient; hard constraints are never dropped; relaxation steps are returned to the client.
- **Cross-encoder re-ranking:** Top-20 fused candidates re-scored jointly with the query and reduced to top-10; degrades gracefully to RRF order if unavailable.
- **Explainable matching:** Deterministic factor-wise score decomposition plus matched / graph-related / missing skills with evidence spans quoted from the vacancy text.
- **Authentication and sessions:** JWT access tokens with rotating refresh tokens, argon2id password hashing, refresh-token reuse detection.
- **Three-tier memory:** Working message window, rolling per-chat LLM summary, and cross-chat long-term structured memory with confidence and provenance. Cross-chat memory defaults to on and can be disabled per account; disabling it does not hide the user's library.
- **Personal library:** Per-user PDF resumes and Markdown/text documents. Hira reads extracted text (pinned files plus a few nearest by embedding). Writes to Markdown/text require confirmation. No separate retrieval microservice.
- **Voice call:** In-app call surface, browser speech, transcript stored outside the chat list. A turn counts against the daily query limit only when it runs retrieval.
- **Favorites and collections:** Saving, annotating, grouping; no application-status tracker (diploma scope).
- **Demo mode:** 1 chat and 3 queries without registration; registered users — unlimited chats, 20 search queries/day (ADR-029); mock Billing/Prices page (monthly/yearly).
- **Resume parsing:** PDF upload into the library → LLM structured extraction → user-confirmed profile update. The file stays in the library.
- **Out of coursework:** MCP tool architecture and auto-apply (background on new vacancies, or on demand for a vacancy already shown in chat). See `Wiki/11-hira.md`.
- **Data validation:** Strict typing of all inbound payloads; shared DTOs and enums in `packages/contracts`.

